Z||000033||Eric||06-08-99||06:43 PM||||Grizco, I tried to drive my GT last winter in less than an inch of snow a kept losing control.  I nearly spun coming up to a stop sign and about 1/8 mile later, I almost lost it under power after shifting into third.  I'm not a bad snow/ice driver either, I just can't seem to engage the clutch smooth enough so that the 170ftlbs don't break the tires loose(nearly bald at the time).<p>I was just in Alaska last September.  There is hardly any place to drive!  A few highways but the rest were seasonal dirt roads.  Owning a Fiero up there would drive me crazy, where could you let it out?
Z||000034||Punkndrublic||06-08-99||11:40 PM||||Yellow85<p>There a red NSX I always see driv'n around where I live hopefully I ll have a chance to race him and I ll tell ya how it is. Otherwise I ll wait 8 months and race my friend when he gets his.<p><br>Does everyone have troubles driving in the snow? My cars standard and I had no problems in the slushy icey snow i get here or the occasional powder, even on slight hills. 4x4s would get stuck in the snow I just drove through, that happened twice. I even went into fresh 1 foot deep snow in parking lots and did donuts for 30 mins and still no problems, I gave up cause I didn't have any fun losing control. My tires aren't even all season not alone winter tires.
Z||000035||grizco||06-08-99||11:48 PM||||Eric <br>Where in Alaska were you? If you were in Anchorage forget it there's no where to open<br>it up and see what the car can do.Most of the time up here in the Fairbanks and North Pole area the best place to drive fast is on the highway going to Delta Junction or Anchorage You can usually average around 85. Except now during the tourest season and the winter time. So i guess in reality theres only about three months of the year when you can go faster than 75. 
Z||000036||Eric||06-09-99||10:08 AM||||I spent most of a week on the Kenai Peninsula.  Never made it up to Fairbanks.  Saw Mt McKinley and bypassed Fairbanks on highway 8 (7 1/2 hours to go 130 miles).  Higway 4 south of Delta Junction was in really bad shape.  I think it was built on permafrost.  It made me seasick.  I couldn't imagine going 85 on that road.
Z||000037||FieroMike||06-10-99||09:55 AM||||Eric,<br>I'm not in to Porches whatsoever so I couldn't tell you if it was a 911,944, whatever.  I know they weren't Turbo, and I know I beat them.  So I would have to agree with what you said about 944 if your information is accurate.  Another thing is, if the car doesn't beat you, it loses, no matter what the reason, whether it's a sorry driver or not.  That's part of racing, if that didn't count you could just match them up in a magazine, now that would be fun.  It wouldn't suprise me that the type of person in a Porche couldn't drive it. Plus, no way can a NA laser,eclipse,talon give my GT a run for it's money.<p>Yellow85,<br>The cheapest mod to get higher top-end is a coil and module, maybe some plugs, a HD cap and rotor.  That should get you going at the top end.
Z||000038||Eric||06-10-99||07:26 PM||||Fiero Mike,<br>What kind of GT do you have?  I don't doubt your driving ability, but there's just some things you can't drive your way out of.  One of these things is a strait line street race.  My 88 GT will go 0-60 in 8 seconds flat (my best run).  Some V6s have been known to break 7 seconds, but this is rare for a car that is stock.  As you may already know, there are a lot of cars out there which can beat this time quite easily.  Such cars include 911s.  If in fact you did have a fair race (other driver was trying to win also), there is no doubt in my mind that you would have lost.  The modern day 911s (mid 80s on) consistently run 0-60 under 6 seconds.  However, if your car is modified and has more than 200 horsepower, I would have to retract my assertion.  The race would have, in fact, been a more balanced one as far as equiptment is concerned.  It is my personal goal to have a 3.2 GT by the end of the summer which will break that 6 second barrier.  I would like to know what mods. you have done so that I can use this information to my advantage.  Who knows, maybe will both be spankin' those Porsches before too long.  
Z||000039||lowCG||06-10-99||09:10 PM||||That's pretty much all I've ever hoped for in my Fiero,and I know that it'll be a whole lot of fun spanking a 911,and not spending nearly as much for the G.C.F.(German Car Factor),which includes high insurance rates, high maintenance,high anxiety,etc.<br>I'm confident with the handling,now beginning to think about putting a turbo off of an '80s T-Bird,with a light amount(7-9 #) of boost,no cat. converter,2 1/2" exhaust.Maybe even on a 3.4,4valve motor if it isn't reliable/fast enough the other way.<br>I've seen cars pass smog without converters('92 model year car standards)as long as they were turbo'd.<br>Oh,yeah,I chased some guy in a Lotus Esprit Turbo once,but it got pretty boring 'cuz I was behind him and couldn't pass on the windy road.
Z||000040||FieroMike||06-11-99||05:41 PM||||Eric,<br>Let me re-iterate that I don't know if it was a 911 or not, and he was trying to race for sure, so it probably wasn't a 911Ouch!  I just bought my Fiero about 2 months ago and it ran pretty strong, I think it was rebuilt even though the rod bearing just went out on cylinder 2.  Once I took everything apart it was all stock no cam, so here are the mods:<p>K&N intake under wheel well<br>MSD module and coil<br>HD cap and rotor<br>Rebuilt Distributer<br>Bosche Platinum Plugs<br>Ported Exhaust Manifolds<br>De-Carbonized the intake<br>ported and polished the intake, upper,middle and lower<br>ADS super chip<br>91 octane gas<p>That's it, after I get this rebuild done it will have the mid-performance cam, TRW pistons, 3.1 liters, new chrome molly rings, new timing chain and sprockets, new WP,ALT,STARTER, all that good stuff and the Power Pully.<p>With the G-Tech the best 0-60 that I got was 6.1 seconds on a pretty level straight-away.  I had a 7.0 flat when I first got the car.  Don't forget my car weighs less than yours because the '85gt isn't a fast-back.  This is also with the 5-speed.  By the time we get our rebuilds done I hope we are spanking those Porche's, if we can't, time for some NOS!!!  What state are you in?<br>Later,<br>Mike
Z||000041||Eric||06-11-99||06:47 PM||||FieroMike,<br>Don't worry.  I'm not pissed at you.  I just get a little defensive when someone says something bad about a Porsche 911.  I knew a guy who restored them for a living.  He was an Indy car driver and used to take me for rides in some of his cars.  These were "older" 911s (1960s-1980s cars).  I know what these things can do.  I also know my 88 GT cannot hang with them, atleast, not with an ex-Indy car driver in the 911.  <p>I hope to order the parts for my rebuild next week before I go on vacation.  I plan on getting 3.1 crank (981 casting), 3.1 Camaro pistons (don't know the part #), GM performance cam with springs, maybe a power pulley, and some other high performance goodies.  I am going to get the heads done, port the intake and exaust manifolds, and put a Centerforce clutch in (the old one is dead).<p>I live in Columbus, Ohio (Indy car capital of the world), FieroMike.
